e're in a world with  very long supply chains supply chains  are unknown and are actively kept away  from us so how can we make sustainable  decisions we can't because we don't have  the information and this is kind of why  instead of focusing on what every  individual can make i ask for collective  policy decisions that can make sure that  companies can actually create products  that are in the lines of ethics that all  of us stand behind  stu do you agree with that as consumers  we have very little power to make green  choices and we need to rely on a  framework that companies have to follow  in order to make sure that their  offerings are green or at least it's  transparent  look i think transparency really helps  us here i think daiser talked about the  complex supply chains and as a primary  producer if you like the first step in  those supply chains the closer i can get  to the consumer the closer consumers can  understand what i do on my farm how i'm  contributing to net zero is really  important and i think too often i think  the big scandal at the moment is the  whole issue of green washing i'm seeing  way too much of this i'm not seeing  people make the the deep cuts we need to  in terms of fossil fuels and i'm not  seeing many other sectors of the economy  stepping up  as i know agriculture is and farmers are  and i think there's still a long way to  go well yes and days this report is  critical of some companies they they  think they're over using this idea of  carbon offsetting perhaps planting a  forest for example so that it allows  them to pollute a little bit more but is  that such a bad thing given there's so  much deforestation and given that trees  can play an important part in tackling  climate change do you think they're  right to be criticized for using that  tool  i think it's very easy to say that  you'll plant a couple of trees as you  continue to pollute the world that we  live in um i think there should be like  an active effort to stopping the harm  that you're creating first and then  thinking of offsetting later  and even when we start talking about  tree initiatives there's a whole grace  phase of if it's not properly done it  can actually cause more harm to the  environment than good um or even for  example the delayed effect of carbon  sequestering it's not within the first i  think some people say 16 years  of a tree being planted before it can  actually start sucking up carbon  effectively so these are the kind of  loopholes that they're using but this  could actually lead us into a place of  unknown and of uncertainty and that's  particularly quite scary it's true i've  seen a lot of head nodding from you  there i mean do you feel that farmers  are affected by this are you seeing that  some farming land is being bought up to  to plant trees and is that such a bad  thing  absolutely and i think it is a bad thing  the wrong tree in the wrong place is is  the wrong thing and for me i am seeing  many situations where uh land is being  taken up by by individuals or by uh  corporations to green wash their  emissions  and at the same time that process is  removing uh family farms people who've  farmed lands for generations and land is  in a limited resource we have to use it  to share  food production and all the other things  we want and at the moment i'm seeing  this this green washing actually taking  away from food production whereas we  should be looking at how we do food  production alongside doing our bit for  climate change as well  okay let's move on to our next topic  shall we because the indian ocean nation  of madagascar has yet again been hit by  extreme weather this time a cyclone  displacing many thousands of people now  we reported from the island not long ago  because of a devastating drought which  has affected huge sections of the  population and these disasters are  happening far away from us here but is  there a message  is this what's coming for us as the  climate change problem continues and  days what do you make of this i mean  obviously  it's a terrible situation for the people  living there but does news of events  like that in places as far away as  madagascar move the dial here do you  think as far as awareness of climate  change is concerned  well i hope it does i think a lot of the  time people forget that  living in the uk we are literally an  island we are very similar to madagascar  in certain senses and we do have the  same vulnerabilities um so there is this  like question of like remembering our  own situation in the world but then also  i think  the reason why a lot of people tend to  not really relate to the harm that's  being caused in places like madagascar  the harm that's being caused in places  like europe is because historically  there's been a devaluing of life in the  global south and when this happens this  means that the natural disasters that  are happening there that if it was  happening in germany we may have empathy  for a lot of people don't give space to  that which is an issue we need to get to  a place in society of where knowing that  someone is suffering and knowing that  someone is hurting is enough for us to  act here regardless of whether it feels  physically close or not stupid do you  agree with that  i i do i i daze speaks with real  authority on this but i think also for  me  let's not pretend these extreme events  aren't happening on our doorstep you  know only two three months ago now i was  on farms in the north of england where  we were seeing uprooted trees we were  seeing destroyed buildings we were  seeing people without power for a long  time and if anyone wants a reminder  about you know climate change being real  impacts both at home and the disastrous  impacts in places like madagascar are a  real uh  shout for everyone a real live call  and are precisely why every single one  of us needs to do our bit to address  climate change well yes and could it  make a difference amongst politicians do  you think days i mean we saw at the last  cop summit in glasgow the vulnerable  nations pushing for more money to adapt  to the effects of climate change that  they're already experiencing do you  think if we see more events during the  course of this year they'll get more  money when it comes to the next cop  well hopefully there are there is money  to cover the loss and damages that are  being faced by these countries but then  also i don't think that our politicians  should be waiting for more  chaos and and hurt to happen before  acting and i think this is where we have  to call on ourselves as active systems  within this democracy  and basically say enough's enough and  say we want to protect all people all  life and we want our governments to put  these policies in place because  a lot of the time people forget that  within democracy the sovereignty and the  power is held within the people and if  we say we need this the government  should be acting accordingly  okay well we must leave it there days of  gargey and stuart roberts always  interesting to get your thoughts thanks  very much indeed for your time tonight  thank you  so that's it for me but uh tomorrow i'll  be joined by deputy director at the  energy and climate intelligence unit  sepi golzari monroe and the journalist  and author david wallis wells do stay  with us coming up next on the great  debate we're asking can you afford the  cost of living crisis that's up next but  for me thanks for watching i'll see you  next time  [Music]
 

